\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs32\b newVM CUDA (cuFFT) vs OpenCL (clFFT) Performance Benchmark\b0\fs22\par
\fs24\b Complex-to-complex FFT/IFFT, single precision, N = 4096 .. 16384\b0\par
\fs24\b REVISION 2 - rerun after newVMCL.pas's plan-caching fix landed\b0\par
\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b Machine:\b0  Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-14650HX (hybrid: 8 P-cores/16 threads + 8 E-cores/8 threads = 24 logical CPUs), 31 GiB RAM\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b OS:\b0  Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS, kernel 7.0.0-30-generic\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b GPU:\b0  NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop GPU, 8151 MiB VRAM, driver 595.84, compute capability 12.0 (Blackwell) - same device/machine as revision 1 of this report\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b Libraries:\b0  CUDA 13.1 (nvcc V13.1.115), cuFFT/cuFFTW from cuda-13-1, OpenCL/clFFT via the same libOpenCL.so.1 / libclFFT.so.2 newVMCL.pas links against, FFTW3 (libfftw3f) - same library newVMComplexSingle.pas uses\par
\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b What changed since revision 1:\b0  Revision 1 of this report (same filename, overwritten here) measured raw clFFT vs cuFFT vs FFTW and found newVMCL.pas's FFT()/IFFT() were rebaking a clFFT plan on EVERY call - a genuinely expensive, driver-side operation (~1-4ms even warm) next to the transform itself (tens of microseconds once a plan exists) - making GPU FFT 12x-220x slower than a single host FFTW call at every size tested. That finding led directly to a fix: newVMCL.pas now caches one baked plan per distinct N (GetFFTPlan/GFFTPlanCache) and reuses it across calls, and also now queries and provides the scratch buffer clfftGetTmpBufSize() says a plan needs (previously always passed as nil). This revision reruns the same raw-library comparison for a fresh baseline, then ADDS real measurements of newVMCL.pas's own FFT()/IFFT() post-fix, to show what actually changed for real callers - not just what an idealized best case could look like.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b Methodology:\b0  Two programs, both committed at the repo root: (1) newVMCUDAvsCLFFTbench.cu (unchanged since revision 1) - a standalone C++/CUDA program exercising cuFFT, clFFT and FFTW directly under IDENTICAL methodology in one process, at N=4096/6144/8192/12288/16384, in two modes per library - "replan/call" (fresh plan every call) and "plan reused" (one plan, executed 100x) - the raw-library ceiling/floor either integration could realistically hit. (2) a new small program built against the ACTUAL newVMCL.pas unit (not a reimplementation), measuring its real FFT()/IFFT() first-call-per-N and 100-repeat-average across the same five sizes - this is what a real caller gets today, plan cache included.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b Correctness:\b0  Round-trip (forward then inverse against the original random input), all libraries, all sizes: residuals ~1e-5 throughout (see revision 1's own Notes for a benchmark-side residual-check bug found and fixed there - not re-litigated here, already resolved). newVMCL.pas's own round trip is additionally now verified with the previously-missing clFFT scratch buffer actually provided (see below) at every size, not just the N=16 newVMTests.pas's TVMobjCLTests covers.\par
\fs22\par
