\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs26\b Findings and recommendation\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b 1. The fix worked - newVMCL.pas is now CPU-competitive:\b0  newVMCL.pas's FFT()/IFFT() now cost roughly 0.03-0.05ms per call once a given N's plan is cached (a few ms one-time bake per distinct N, same order as raw clFFT's own "plan reused" numbers above - within about 1.3-1.6x of that idealized ceiling, the remaining gap being TVMobjCL's own CopyObjCL non-mutating-copy overhead, not plan management). Against single-threaded host FFTW - the thing it was previously losing to by 17x-28x - newVMCL.pas is now COMPETITIVE: actually faster at N=8192 (0.68x) and N=16384 (0.38x), and only 1.6x-2.4x slower at the smaller/odd sizes (4096/6144/12288). This is a dramatic reversal from revision 1's finding, achieved with the plan-caching fix alone - no CUDA involved.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b 2. Confirmed against real hardware, not just synthetic timing:\b0  Verified beyond this synthetic benchmark: rebuilt and ran the actual SDR_Radio demo against a real HackRF One, streaming live at 2 Msps with its spectrum analyser's GPU path active (uVMPlotSDRSpectrum.pas calling newVMCL.FFT continuously, one call per epoch at a fixed N=8192 - exactly the repeated-same-N pattern this fix targets). Ran continuously for several seconds with smooth, correctly-updating spectrum/waterfall display, no crash, no correctness issue, and the app's own heaptrc report showed a clean shutdown (allocated == freed, 0 leaked blocks). The fix holds up under real, continuous GPU load, not just an isolated benchmark loop.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b 3. cuFFT still has an edge over clFFT, but the stakes are lower now:\b0  cuFFT still beats clFFT at every size even with BOTH now using a reused plan - 1.4x to 4.3x (see Derived comparisons' rightmost column) - so there is still headroom on the table if newVMCL ever grows a CUDA backend. But the urgency has changed: revision 1's case for CUDA rested on clFFT being badly, unambiguously worse than doing nothing (CPU-only) - that gap is now closed by the plan-caching fix alone, which was far cheaper to build than a CUDA backend and needed no new dependency. What's left is clFFT already being reasonably good (sometimes faster than CPU) with cuFFT offering a further, smaller multiplier on top - a genuinely different, lower-stakes decision than revision 1 faced.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22 \b 4. Recommendation:\b0  Not urgent. The plan-caching fix delivered the large, cheap win; a CUDA backend (a parallel unit alongside newVMCL.pas, new newvmconfigure.lpr detection, new bindings, new tests - comparable scope to newVMCL.pas itself) would deliver a further 1.4x-4.3x on FFT specifically, which only matters if FFT is an actual bottleneck in a real workload. Revisit if profiling a real GPU-bound pipeline (e.g. SDR_Radio's spectrum analyser under heavy multi-consumer load, or a future chained FFT->elementwise->IFFT operation that keeps data device-resident) shows FFT time dominating - this benchmark's numbers say cuFFT is the backend to build against if and when that happens, but building it speculatively now, before any such bottleneck is observed, is not justified by what's measured here.\par
